'''Luzamontaña''' ({{Literal translation|bright mountain}}; {{Langx|mxi|لُوزَامُونْتَانْيَا|Luzamońtańa}}; {{Langx|gug|Wakárapahirí|4=painted mountains}}) is one of the [[regions of Balisca|eight regions]] that make up the [[Araucaria|Araucarian community]], within the [[Baliscan Confederation]]. With an area of {{convert|86519.36|km2|abbr=on}} it is the third-largest region within Araucaria, and the [[List of Baliscan regions by area|fifth-largest region in Balisca]] overall. It is bordered to north by [[Hesperia|Hesperia's]] [[Piedmont]], the east by the [[Castejón Metropolis]] and the region of [[Tukupi]], and to the south by [[Brasal]]. The region's administrative seat and largest city is [[Jatiel]]. The region's name stems from the widespread presence of [[W:Firefly|fireflies]] (specifically [[W:Photinus signaticollis|''photinus signaticollis'']]) observed by early settlers.

Evidence of [[Indigenous peoples in Balisca|Indigenous]] habitation in the area dates from at least 19,000 years ago and successive waves of arrivals led to one of the most densely populated areas of pre-colonial [[Abyala]]. The arrival of Volisanian and Burawi explorers in the early 16th century and the consolidation of the [[Hesperian League]] did not result in the establishment of permanent settlements due to the hostility of the Indigenous peoples. 

Luzamontaña is separated from [[Hesperia]] by the [[Guadalquivir]], a strait that connects [[Salto Bay]] to the [[Aethiopian Ocean]]. As the maritime gateway to the [[Castejón Metropolis]] and the wider [[Concordia]] area, Luzamontaña is a major economic center, transportation hub, and tourist destination in Balisca, with 60% of the region's land area designated as {{W|protected area|protected conservation areas}}. Since the 1990s, Luzamontaña has seen substantial population and economic growth as a result of continued socioeconomic decentralization of the [[Concordia|Concordia conurbation]].

